WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.720
13.6 billion years ago.

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:09.230
The dark scaffold that supports all the
regular matter in the universe emerges ablaze

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.340
with stars.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:17.930
But how did this stellar array evolve into a
structure littered with organized galaxies?

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.270
It seems they formed under constant threat
of destruction.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:34.600
October 2020, astronomers discover a monster
lurking among the Cosmic Web's earliest

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.550
structures dating to 900 million years after
the Big Bang.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:42.010
A supermassive black hole.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:49.660
Six galaxies surround this cosmic giant
caught in its grip, seemingly linked to the

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:55.090
supermassive black hole by filaments of the
developing cosmic web.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:01.660
It's like the universe has given supermassive
black holes an umbilical cord.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:04.840
It's like an all you can eat buffet right
there.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:10.540
Supermassive black holes are hungry beasts.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:14.230
They feast on any matter that gets too close
to them.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:20.930
Supermassive black holes are likely some of
the most powerful objects in the universe.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.780
They can be anywhere between 100,000 to 10
billion times the mass of the sun.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:33.620
Supermassive black holes have been a nemesis
for generations of scientists, not because of

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:40.550
their fearsome nature, but because nobody
knows how they grew so large so early.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.910
I wish I knew where supermassive black holes
came from.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:47.180
If I knew, I would have a Nobel Prize
hanging around my neck and I would wear it

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:48.230
every single day.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:56.290
As someone who deeply loves supermassive
black holes, whose career is based on

00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:01.300
studying supermassive black holes, it is
very frustrating to not know where they come

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:01.990
from.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:09.910
Regular stellar black holes are the collapsed
cores of dead stars ranging from 3 to 10

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.080
solar masses.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:16.030
But supermassive black holes, those are a
different beast.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:23.890
13 billion years ago, not enough stars had
lived and died to build something as huge as

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:25.810
a supermassive black hole.

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:34.240
Now, the cosmic Web offers scientists clues
about the black hole conundrum.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:41.620
We now know supermassive black holes grow
among the lattice of the young cosmic web,

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.120
gorging on the hydrogen gas that travels
along the filaments.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.860
At the same time, when the cosmic web is
lighting up, supermassive black holes appear

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:56.380
to be stealing star fuel from the young
universe.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.190
You might think that would kill a growing
galaxy.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:06.250
And yet, most mature galaxies have a
supermassive black hole.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.500
They really dominate the physics of what
happens in the centers of galaxies and even

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.390
how galaxies can evolve.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.490
We think these galactic monsters have been
around from the start.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:25.000
How, then, did the Web's young galaxies
develop around supermassive black holes?

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:31.550
The Milky Way's supermassive black hole is
called Sagittarius a star.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:38.480
It's around 27 million miles wide and weighs
in at just over 4 million solar masses.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.710
The environment around Sagittarius A-star is
very dynamic.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.990
It can actually be a really hellish place.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:55.020
There's this accretion disk that's full of
plasma is heated to thousands of degrees, so

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:59.940
you wouldn't necessarily think that that's a
great place for star formation to happen.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:05.010
But that's exactly where astronomers decided
to look.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:10.760
Using the Atacama Large Millimeter Array or
ALMA for short.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:17.710
Scientists scanned the heart of the Milky
Way for dense cores of gas and dust stellar

00:04:17.710 --> 00:04:26.140
embryos. They found more than 800 within
just 1000 light years of Sagittarius, a star,

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:32.180
including more than 40 embryos with
energetic jets blasting from their cores.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.440
The telltale sign of the birth of stars.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.790
It's really surprising to find those stars
there.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:45.330
It's like hearing baby's cries from a wolf's
den.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:53.230
It's not the place you would expect this to
happen, but in fact, stars are forming there

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:57.340
now. It's not as efficient as it is out here
in the suburbs where things are quieter.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:58.870
But it works.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:06.630
Baby stars igniting and thriving around a
supermassive black hole, the kind of hostile

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.990
environment we know existed in the young
Cosmic Web.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:14.880
Starbirth is a key part of kickstarting
young galaxies.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:21.450
This evidence suggests that star formation
is more resilient than researchers thought,

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:25.560
and they've developed a theory to explain
it.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:32.010
Gas and dust race around the black hole in
the accretion disk heated to incredible

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:36.660
temperatures, plumes of gas break off and
blast into space.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:41.640
The gas rapidly cools, collapses and forms
baby stars.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:46.620
These accretion disks are the most chaotic
of stellar nurseries.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:52.410
You see this mechanism that you think is
violently inhibiting star formation, and at

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.380
the same time, it's triggering the birth of
new stars.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:02.380
Matter clumps at the cosmic webs
intersections feeding the supermassive black

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:04.470
holes around them.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:08.350
Stars burst into life slowly building
galaxies.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:16.360
This could be how our own Milky Way formed
among the filaments of the young cosmic web.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:23.710
But new research suggests that growth in
these baby galaxies requires murder and

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:27.910
mayhem. And without them, we wouldn't exist.

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:31.360
The universe does need to churn things up.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:32.380
You need to break some eggs.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:36.700
To make an omelet, you need to introduce
some chaos into your galaxy to rapidly form

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:38.530
stars or grow black holes.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:44.250
Smashing things together is how the universe
came to be.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:53.220
The Hubble Space Telescope discovers many
distorted galaxies twisted, battered and torn

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:57.180
victims of violent collisions on a cosmic
scale.

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:02.950
Galaxies are never sitting quietly doing
nothing.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:05.500
They're always undergoing change.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:10.630
They're constantly encountering and slamming
into and colliding with and mixing with other

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:11.650
galaxies.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:17.740
You can see images in Hubble of total car
wrecks of galaxies that are trying to merge

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:18.670
with each other.

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:23.620
We know that galaxies collide now.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:29.260
But what about in the early universe when
the cosmic web was beginning to take shape?

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.080
Astronomers study a strange galaxy named
Himiko.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:43.110
Born just 800 million years after the Big
Bang, three bright light sources suggest

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:44.910
intense star formation.

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:52.770
Detailed analysis reveals not one galaxy,
but three baby galaxies not yet fully formed.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:08:00.130
Scientists call these youthful star systems
proto galaxies.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:03.610
The trio that make up himiko are in mid
collision.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:12.010
Computer simulations of the early universe
suggest Protogalaxies smashed together with

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:13.390
frightening regularity.

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:18.070
These violent shakeups trigger star birth.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:24.900
Protogalaxies are rich in gas and when they
collide and merge, those gas clouds collide

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:28.770
and collapse and form stars, sometimes at
prodigious rates.

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:33.630
And after a billion years or so, all of that
structure forms and you get a formal galaxy.

